%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: factors - teacher development
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s belief in their own ability to help all students
      learn is known as:'
    Options:
    - Self-esteem
    - Teacher efficacy
    - Personal motivation
    - Academic confidence
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a personal factor affecting teacher development?
    Options:
    - School leadership
    - The national curriculum
    - A teacher's level of reflectiveness
    - Availability of teaching resources
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A school where teachers regularly observe each other''s classes
      and provide feedback has a:'
    Options:
    - Isolated culture
    - Collaborative culture
    - Competitive culture
    - Traditional culture
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who is driven by a genuine passion for their subject
      matter is influenced by:'
    Options:
    - Extrinsic motivation
    - Intrinsic motivation
    - Administrative pressure
    - Parental expectations
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which contextual factor can severely limit the time a teacher has
      for professional development?
    Options:
    - Strong teacher self-efficacy
    - High levels of student motivation
    - A reflective disposition
    - Excessive administrative workload
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'The ability of a teacher to bounce back from a difficult lesson
      and try again is an example of:'
    Options:
    - Openness to change
    - Resilience
    - High academic qualification
    - External motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'The role of a supportive principal in teacher development is primarily
      to:'
    Options:
    - Increase administrative duties
    - Provide instructional leadership and resources
    - Focus solely on student discipline
    - Minimize teacher collaboration
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s personal philosophy about how children learn best
      is a(n):'
    Options:
    - Contextual factor
    - Administrative policy
    - Personal belief about teaching and learning
    - Extrinsic motivator
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a contextual factor?
    Options:
    - A teacher's subject knowledge
    - The socio-economic background of the students
    - A teacher's emotional intelligence
    - A teacher's commitment to the profession
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who regularly analyzes their lessons to see what worked
      and what didn''t is engaging in:'
    Options:
    - Complaining
    - Reflective practice
    - External motivation
    - Isolation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'Professional Development (PD) is most effective when it is:'
    Options:
    - A one-time, lecture-based workshop
    - Ongoing, relevant to classroom needs, and includes follow-up support
    - Mandatory and unrelated to teacher interests
    - Focused only on theoretical knowledge
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: A teacher's family responsibilities and health are examples of
      _____ factors.
    Options:
    - Contextual
    - Policy-related
    - Personal and circumstantial
    - Societal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A school policy that emphasizes high-stakes standardized testing
      can:'
    Options:
    - Unquestionably promote teacher creativity.
    - Limit teacher autonomy and hinder developmental risk-taking.
    - Be the only factor in teacher development.
    - Replace the need for teacher motivation.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: A teacher's depth of knowledge in their subject area is a _____
      factor.
    Options:
    - Contextual
    - Personal
    - Societal
    - Administrative
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "Professional Learning Communities" (PLCs) directly
      supports teacher development by fostering:'
    Options:
    - Isolation and independent work
    - Strict adherence to textbooks
    - Competition for resources
    - Collaboration and shared responsibility
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who is willing to try a new teaching strategy even if
      it might fail demonstrates:'
    Options:
    - Low self-efficacy
    - Openness to change and risk-taking
    - Resistance to development
    - Poor planning
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Lack of access to basic teaching-learning materials (TLMs) is a
      _____ barrier to teacher development.
    Options:
    - Personal
    - Contextual-resource based
    - Motivational
    - Philosophical
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s motivation that comes from a desire for promotion
      or a salary increment is:'
    Options:
    - Intrinsic motivation
    - Self-efficacy
    - Reflectiveness
    - Extrinsic motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'The emotional environment of the school, characterized by trust
      and respect, is part of the:'
    Options:
    - National education policy
    - School climate
    - Teacher's personal life
    - Student curriculum
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s ability to understand and manage their own emotions
      in a stressful classroom is an aspect of:'
    Options:
    - Subject knowledge
    - Emotional intelligence
    - Extrinsic motivation
    - Administrative skill
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which factor can either be a major challenge or an opportunity
      for developing resilience, depending on support?
    Options:
    - Teaching in a high-needs, resource-poor environment
    - Having a strong academic background
    - Being an intrinsically motivated teacher
    - Having a small class size
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A punitive teacher evaluation system is likely to:'
    Options:
    - Foster innovation and creativity.
    - Create fear and inhibit professional growth.
    - Have no impact on teacher development.
    - Increase intrinsic motivation.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s life stage (e.g., being a new parent) can influence
      their development by affecting their:'
    Options:
    - Subject knowledge
    - Available time and energy for professional activities
    - Core teaching beliefs
    - Understanding of school policy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: The societal status accorded to teachers is a _____ factor influencing
      their morale and development.
    Options:
    - Personal
    - Contextual-societal
    - Administrative
    - Resource-based
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who believes "students cannot learn this difficult topic"
      is constrained by their:'
    Options:
    - High self-efficacy
    - Limiting beliefs about student learning
    - Strong resource support
    - Collaborative school culture
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a personal disposition that positively
      affects development?
    Options:
    - A fixed mindset
    - Resistance to feedback
    - Curiosity and a love for learning
    - Blaming students for failure
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: The "input" that a teacher brings from their own education and
      training is a _____ factor.
    Options:
    - Contextual
    - Personal
    - Policy
    - Societal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A key role of school leadership in teacher development is to create:'
    Options:
    - A culture of fear and competition
    - Conditions that support learning and growth for both teachers and students
    - An overwhelming amount of paperwork
    - Strict, unchangeable rules
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s development is most sustainable when it is driven
      by:'
    Options:
    - Only extrinsic rewards
    - A combination of intrinsic motivation and supportive contexts
    - Fear of punishment
    - Mandatory policy directives
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Large class sizes primarily act as a _____ barrier to teacher development.
    Options:
    - Personal-motivational
    - Philosophical
    - Contextual-structural
    - Resource-based
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s reflection-on-action occurs:'
    Options:
    - While they are teaching
    - After the teaching has taken place
    - Before they plan a lesson
    - Never
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which factor is most directly under a teacher's own control?
    Options:
    - National education policy
    - Their own level of reflectiveness and effort to learn
    - The school's budget for resources
    - Societal attitudes towards education
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A school that provides time for teachers to plan and learn together
      is investing in a supportive:'
    Options:
    - Personal factor
    - Philosophical factor
    - Contextual factor
    - Fixed mindset
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who feels trusted and respected by their principal is
      more likely to:'
    Options:
    - Avoid taking any risks
    - Experiment with new teaching methods
    - Isolate themselves from colleagues
    - Focus only on textbook teaching
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: "The factor of \"teacher identity\" \u2013 how one sees oneself
      as a professional \u2013 is a _____ factor."
    Options:
    - Contextual
    - Personal
    - Resource-based
    - Administrative
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A prescriptive, centralized curriculum can hinder teacher development
      by reducing:'
    Options:
    - Student enrollment
    - The cost of education
    - Teacher autonomy and creativity
    - Parental involvement
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s ability to perceive and understand the emotions of
      their students is a component of:'
    Options:
    - Subject Matter Knowledge
    - Emotional Intelligence
    - Extrinsic Motivation
    - Administrative Efficiency
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which combination is MOST conducive to continuous teacher development?
    Options:
    - Low self-efficacy and a punitive school climate.
    - High intrinsic motivation and a collaborative school culture.
    - Extrinsic motivation only and abundant resources.
    - Fixed beliefs about learning and a supportive principal.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'The "Matthew Effect" in teacher development suggests that:'
    Options:
    - All teachers develop at the same rate.
    - Contextual factors are the only thing that matters.
    - Personal factors are irrelevant.
    - Teachers who start strong and get support tend to get stronger, while those
      who struggle may fall further behind.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s personal circumstances, such as a health issue, can
      impact their development by affecting their:'
    Options:
    - Core teaching philosophy
    - Energy and focus at work
    - Understanding of the curriculum
    - Relationships with the principal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A key difference between personal and contextual factors is that
      personal factors are:'
    Options:
    - Always fixed and unchangeable
    - Internal to the teacher, while contextual factors are external
    - Less important than contextual factors
    - The same for every teacher in a school
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A school''s decision to fund a teacher''s participation in a national
      conference is an example of supporting development through:'
    Options:
    - Personal motivation
    - Mandating a personal disposition
    - Changing the teacher's beliefs
    - Contextual resource allocation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who believes "I can learn to be better at teaching students
      with disabilities" has a:'
    Options:
    - Fixed mindset
    - Growth mindset
    - Low self-efficacy
    - Resistance to change
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: The factor of "workload" is primarily a _____ factor.
    Options:
    - Personal
    - Contextual
    - Philosophical
    - Motivational (only intrinsic)
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'Teacher development is best understood as a process influenced
      by:'
    Options:
    - Personal factors only.
    - Contextual factors only.
    - The dynamic interaction between personal and contextual factors.
    - Pre-service training only.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: A teacher's personal value system and ethics are a _____ factor
      affecting their professional choices.
    Options:
    - Contextual
    - Personal
    - Resource-based
    - Policy-related
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: A national policy that mandates 50 hours of professional development
      per year is a _____ factor.
    Options:
    - Personal
    - Contextual-policy
    - Philosophical
    - Motivational (only extrinsic)
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher''s sense of satisfaction when a struggling student finally
      understands a concept is a form of:'
    Options:
    - Extrinsic reward
    - Administrative feedback
    - Contextual pressure
    - Intrinsic motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a barrier to development created by the
      context?
    Options:
    - A teacher's lack of reflectiveness
    - A school culture that discourages collaboration and innovation
    - A teacher's low self-efficacy
    - A teacher's fixed mindset
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - teacher development
